<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herbert Baker</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1862">1862</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Herbert Baker was an English architect who worked mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. In South Africa he designed the Union Buildings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a> he worked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edwin-lutyens">Edwin Lutyens</a>, designing the two Secretariat buildings and the circular Parliament House. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> his works include the rebuilding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bank-of-england">Bank of England</a>, criticised for destroying much of John Soane's earlier building.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herbert-baker.jpg" alt="Herbert Baker" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles Herbert Reilly · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Representative_British_Architects_of_the_Present_Day_1931_(147596964)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Baker was born on 9 June 1862 at Owletts, a country house in Kent, one of nine children of the gentleman farmer Thomas Henry Baker and Frances Davis. The stone of Kent's Norman cathedrals and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> churches shaped his later work. Educated at Tonbridge School, he was articled in 1879 to his cousin, the architect Arthur Baker, and studied at the Architectural Association School and the Royal Academy Schools, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edwin-lutyens">Edwin Lutyens</a> was a classmate.</p><p>In 1891 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> to help his brother Lionel set up a fruit farm on land owned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes">Cecil Rhodes</a>. Rhodes asked him to rebuild his fire-damaged home, Groote Schuur, and in 1900 sent him on a tour of ancient sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> to prepare him for public buildings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Baker became diocesan architect for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> in 1893 and in 1899 went into partnership with Francis Edward Masey; many Cape Town buildings credited to Baker before 1910 were in fact Masey's. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> died in 1902, the High Commissioner Alfred Milner brought him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a> for post-war building, making him one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milners-kindergarten">Milner's Kindergarten</a>. He left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> in 1912 once the Union Buildings were complete.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a> a bitter and public feud followed when the siting of his Secretariat blocked the view of Lutyens's Viceroy's House. From 1918 Baker, Lutyens and Reginald Blomfield were Principal Architects to the Imperial War Graves Commission. Baker completed 112 cemeteries, extensions and memorials in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, one cemetery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, Tyne Cot, and 24 memorials in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. From 1925 he spent five years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a>, where the plan of Nairobi laid out <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/racial-segregation">racial segregation</a> and he designed Government House, the Supreme Court Building and Nairobi City Hall.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bank-of-england">Bank of England</a>, his last building, kept Soane's outer walls and reused his columns and friezes, but critics called it pompous, and Nikolaus Pevsner called the demolition of Soane's building the worst architectural crime in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">City of London</a> in the 20th century. Baker retired at 76 to Owletts, which he left to the National Trust, and published his autobiography, Architecture &amp; Personalities, in 1944. Knighted in 1926, he received the Royal Institute of British Architects' Royal Gold Medal in 1927. He died on 4 February 1946, and despite opposition from the Royal Academy of Arts he was buried in the nave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Herbert Baker” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cultural life flourished among the new middle classes: Rabindranath Tagore won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1913, and the architecture of New Delhi, designed by Edwin Lutyens and <b>Herbert Baker</b> and inaugurated in 1931, still houses the government of India.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Owletts, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Owletts, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.39,0.39&amp;year=1916" aria-label="Owletts on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4559 384 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Herbert Baker"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5009 654h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5009 654h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.39,0.39&amp;year=1916">What was here in 1916? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
